            <ArticleTitle>Myospasm of internal anal sphincter in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>671-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To introduce a common but not well-recognized acute abdominal disease in children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty-eight children with spasm of the internal anal sphincter were reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The main symptom was the sudden onset of continuous pain in the lower abdomen with paroxysmal exacerbation. The typical sign was the dilation of the sigmoid colon with tenderness aggravated by pressure. Plain X-ray films showed dilation of the sigmoid colon and rectum. The symptoms could be relieved by digital rectal examination or a glycerin suppository.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It is the temporary achalasia of the internal anal sphincter that causes acute abdominal pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
